nce to state that while  he agreed with the majority opinion that  there was never a due process right to  abortion the court should also make it  clear that there was no such thing as  substantive due process at all justice  thomas believes that the constitution's  due process clause only addresses the  processes the government uses before  taking a person's life liberty or  property and it has nothing to say about  the substance of the person's rights he  left no doubt about what he thought  about rights to gay marriage  contraception and same-sex intimate  conduct quote in future cases we should  reconsider all of the court's  substantive due process precedents  including griswold lawrence and  obergefell though critics noted that  thomas did not address the similar case  of loving versus virginia the case that  legalized interracial marriage and  protects the legitimacy of thomas's own  marriage it's hard to square a deeply  rooted analysis with keeping loving  versus virginia intact if the court is  going to go after the rest of the  unenumerated rights cases however loving  was also grounded in equal protection it  is related to but not totally dependent  on these substantive due process cases  he wants to overturn  that takes us to the descent of justices  breyer sotomayor and kagan  the dissenters critiqued the majorities  claim that they were simply returning  the issue to state legislatures where it  belongs quote we believe in a  constitution that puts some issues off  limits to majority rule in other words  the dissenting opinion warns that it is  dangerous for the majority to have total  control over minority rights the dissent  rejects alito's claim that rowan casey  just randomly appeared that they came  from nowhere and went nowhere this they  write is not true the dissenting opinion  takes issue with alito's statement that  casey and roe didn't allow courts to  even consider whether the state has an  interest in promoting life and the  dissent says 